Rules:
- Pick the single most specific primitive_key that fits the bug.
- Keep `surface` and `reproducer_carrier` separate. A shipped command-line
  program reading a testcase may be only the carrier for a library or format
  boundary. Record the boundary source review establishes; never infer
  `file-format` merely from `cli + bytes`. If the tool's own options or command
  handling are vulnerable, both may be `cli`.
- `caller_contract` is `violated` when the public documentation states the
  precondition the testcase breaks. Normative wording such as "must make sure",
  "should", ownership/lifetime requirements, or valid parent/child/argument
  types can state a contract even when the implementation does not enforce it.
  Distinguish a requirement that defines valid inputs from a recommendation
  that merely suggests a safer follow-up action. Use `unspecified` when the
  public docs are silent; do not turn silence into `obeyed`.
- Prefer library-api when the surface is unclear but the bug lives in a library API.
- For an open redirect: primitive=open_redirect; surface=library-api (or network for an HTTP service).
- Use caller_controls for what the external caller shapes. Use parameter_control
  and trusted_caller_actions for extra local preconditions; do not repeat bytes
  there when a normal public caller supplies the trigger bytes directly.
- If reproducing the crash requires the caller to install a callback — a memory
  allocator/free or lock hook, a match/substitute callout, a recursion/resource
  guard, a comparator, or an enumeration callback — that frees, destroys, or
  mutates an object the in-flight library call still owns, the precondition is a
  caller call-sequence: set caller_controls to "call-sequence" and
  trusted_caller_actions to "callback ordering". Input bytes may control WHEN the
  callback fires, but the destructive callback is caller-installed code, not
  attacker bytes — so do not classify caller_controls as "bytes".
- If the bug instead needs the caller to issue a SEQUENCE of public API calls
  AFTER parsing — e.g. parse, then sort / delete / move / merge a node, then a
  later call that dereferences the now-stale pointer — and attacker bytes only
  supply the parsed object the sequence acts on (they do NOT choose or drive
  the destructive ordering), the bytes are setup and the proximate trigger is
  the caller's call-sequence. Set caller_controls to "call-sequence",
  trusted_caller_actions to "normal public call", and trigger_source to "both"
  (bytes set up the object; the out-of-attacker-control call-sequence fires the
  bug). A single parse of the right bytes cannot reach the bug, so "bytes"
  alone would over-rate it as attacker-byte-reachable.
- If reaching the fault requires the application or operator to have enabled a
  NON-DEFAULT mode, module, codec, filter, or option — the same bytes do not
  reach it under the target's default invocation — that selection is
  application configuration, not attacker input. Keep caller_controls and
  trigger_source describing the bytes the attacker still shapes, and set
  parameter_control to "application-supplied" so the precondition is scored. A
  path that is on by default needs no such marking.
- Use trigger_source "call-sequence" ONLY for a purely local call-ordering
  trigger with no attacker byte/content path. If input bytes drive the trigger
  at all — including merely controlling callback timing, or when a single
  parse of attacker bytes itself reaches the fault — keep trigger_source
  "bytes" and record any local precondition in caller_controls instead;
  mislabelling a byte-reachable bug as call-sequence (or "both") sends a valid
  security report out of the threat model. Reserve "both" for the case above,
  where attacker bytes are only setup for a caller-driven destructive sequence.
- A fixed, contract-obeying application setup is not automatically a trigger
  component. For example, creating or reusing a context, or enabling a
  supported parser mode, belongs in `parameter_control` and
  `trusted_caller_actions`. Hold that setup fixed and ask whether changing the
  attacker bytes decides whether the fault occurs. If it does, use `bytes`, not
  `both`; if the same fault occurs independently of those bytes, the setup or
  call-sequence is the trigger.
- The same rule covers contract-obeying resource shaping performed before
  input consumption to select a built-in fallback, such as filling a bounded
  cache, pool, or descriptor allowance. When that state is held fixed, do not
  label the calls used to establish it as an attacker-controlled call-sequence.
  If the setup is non-default, record `parameter_control` as
  `application-supplied`; if changing the bytes then decides the fault, keep
  `trigger_source` as `bytes`.
- "env" and "fs-state" are for state the fuzz input does NOT control: "env" is
  process environment variables; "fs-state" is filesystem path/presence/
  permission/symlink layout. The CONTENTS of an attacker-supplied input file,
  argv, stdin, or fuzz corpus are "bytes" (or "both" when they only set up a
  caller-driven sequence) — never "env"/"fs-state". Delivery does not change the
  trust class: reading a testcase via fopen(argv[1]) or stdin does not make its
  bytes environmental or filesystem state.
- For resource/algorithmic-exhaustion primitives (stack_exhaustion,
  dos_amplification, regex_dos, memory_leak, oom), classify trigger_source by
  the exhausting quantity, not the field values. The exhausting quantity is the
  nesting depth, element/iteration count, total size, or backtracking steps that
  drives the blow-up. If the library's own input path provably bounds it below
  the level that exhausts — e.g. a parser nesting or size limit lower than the
  depth or count the crash needs — attacker-supplied bytes cannot reach the
  threshold, and only a trusted caller building the structure through the API
  can: set trigger_source to "call-sequence". Keep trigger_source "bytes" only
  when the quantity is itself attacker-driven through input, e.g. catastrophic
  regex backtracking from an attacker pattern/subject or deep nesting a parser
  accepts with no lower limit. Controlling the field VALUES is not controlling
  the exhausting quantity.
- Use `data_race` ONLY when the report carries a race-detector diagnostic
  (ThreadSanitizer / "WARNING: DATA RACE"). A race argued from source alone —
  unsynchronized shared state, a TOCTOU window, a global cache read across
  threads — is `race_condition`: the consequence is the security decision it
  defeats, not memory corruption. Naming a consequence the report only
  speculates about is worse than either; classify what the report establishes.
- Output JSON only, no prose, no markdown fences.

